WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:20.000
The biggest mistake managers make is jumping in with advice too soon. The Coaching Habit is a approach to keep you curious a little longer and rush to action a little more slowly.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Kickoff question: "What's on your mind?" It opens the conversation without you guessing the topic. Follow with the focus question: "And what else?" — ask it two or three times. Most people stop at the first answer, but the real issue often appears on the second or third "what else."

00:00:46.000 --> 00:01:10.000
When someone is stuck, use the foundation question: "What do you want?" Clarity here prevents half an hour of solving the wrong problem. Then the lazy question: "How can I help?" — it stops you from taking on work that is not yours to do.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:35.000
For strategic coaching, ask "If you are saying yes to this, what are you saying no to?" Trade-offs become visible. When anxiety shows up, try "What was most useful for you?" — it trains them to find their own learning instead of depending on your praise.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Practice exercise: in your next ten-minute one-on-one, use only questions for the first seven minutes. Notice how often you want to advise. The technique is to replace "Here is what I would do" with "What have you already considered?"

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:22.000
When a client cancels sessions repeatedly, explore with "What's the real challenge here for you?" Cancellation is often a symptom — the coaching conversation should address the pattern, not just reschedule the calendar.
